WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.219 --> 00:00:11.510
Congresswoman Katie Porter has really become
very, very good at humiliating people who

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:18.100
seem to think she's clueless, people who go
in to testify before Congress and apparently

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.840
think that they're going to be able to get
one over on her when they are not, because

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.449
she's not only extremely knowledgeable on
all issues, financial and business, but extraordinarily

00:00:26.449 --> 00:00:27.560
well prepared.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:33.690
And there was a subcommittee hearing yesterday
called Modernizing Energy Development Laws

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:37.730
for the benefit of taxpayers, communities
in the environment.

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:39.060
It's a mouthful.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:45.400
One of the people who was testifying was Mark
Murphy, the president of the Strato Production

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.180
Company in Roswell, New Mexico.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.050
This is an oil and gas company.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:52.320
It's not a huge company.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.590
They have 16 employees and generate, according
to a report, about two and a half million

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:58.770
dollars in sales.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:03.000
So it's not a massive company, but not particularly
relevant today.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:09.400
And in an exchange with Katie Porter, Murphy
tries to claim there's no special tax rules

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.290
for oil and gas.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:16.830
If we have expenses, we get to deduct expenses
like any other business, trying to suggest

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:23.210
there aren't special rules that apply to oil
and gas that don't apply to a mom and pop

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:27.360
hardware store or a taco stand or whatever
the case may be.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:28.530
It's not true.

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:31.720
And Katie Porter knew it and she wasn't going
to let it slip.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:32.720
Take a look.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.131
They're estimated to be between 60 to 90 percent
of the costs of drilling.

00:01:37.131 --> 00:01:42.800
How much of those intangible drilling costs
do you go to the doctor right away from your

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:43.800
taxes?

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.920
We get to deduct all of those just like any
other business.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:47.920
We do not.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:48.920
How much do you think I took?

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.659
The tax seems to be a misconception out there
that you're operating from, that somehow the

00:01:52.659 --> 00:01:57.780
oil and gas industry benefits from some special
sort of tax structure.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:03.000
We don't we actually we actually have an industry
group of.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:11.080
I will follow up with you, Mr. Murphy, but
you do benefit from special rules.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:17.620
There's a special tax law for intangible drilling
costs that does not apply to other kinds of

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:18.630
expenses that businesses have.

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:23.849
You get to deduct 70 percent of your costs
immediately and other businesses have to amortize

00:02:23.849 --> 00:02:26.950
their expenses over their entire profits.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:32.030
We also Prisco, please don't patronize me
by telling me of the oil and gas industry

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:36.150
doesn't have any special tax provisions, because
if you would like that to be the rule, I would

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:39.780
be happy to help Congress deliver.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:40.780
I yield back.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:43.720
So it actually goes way beyond just this.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:50.150
The truth is that there are many tax benefits
available for oil and gas investors that aren't

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:53.930
available elsewhere in the tax code for other
types of businesses.

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:58.830
And some of them are sort of relatively not
so much esoteric, but because they only apply

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:05.160
to to a very narrow scope of business, a lot
of people aren't aware of them.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:12.330
The two largest subsidies for oil and gas
are something called excess of percentage

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:18.250
over cost depletion and the expensing of exploration
and development costs.

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:22.500
These add up to billions in subsidies for
companies like Mark Murphys.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:27.830
And when we talk about excessive percentage
over cost depletion, what this allows producers

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:36.069
a harvester's of oil and gas to do is deduct
a fixed percentage of gross revenue as a capital

00:03:36.069 --> 00:03:42.110
expense each year, regardless of whether they
actually had that capital expense or not.

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:49.030
So when you see the way that you would you
would typically deduct costs is you deduct

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:50.470
real costs.

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:57.280
You have a million dollars in revenue and
one hundred thousand dollars in costs, and

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:00.269
you deduct one hundred thousand dollars in
real costs.

00:04:00.269 --> 00:04:09.401
This special rule for oil and gas allows producers
like Mark Murphy's Company to deduct 15 percent

00:04:09.401 --> 00:04:15.900
of gross revenue for what's called resource
depletion, regardless of whether they actually

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:17.900
incurred that as an amount of cost.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:21.070
You just chop 15 percent right off the top.

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:22.810
You made 10 million.

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:28.120
You get to deduct one point five million,
15 percent because you are depleting your

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:29.730
source of oil and gas.

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:31.520
You get a benefit.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:39.040
From the very depletion of a resource which
you are executing, what that is a specific

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:46.090
benefit for oil and gas, intangible drilling
costs, which include everything but actual

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:47.290
drilling equipment.

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:54.840
So when you when you do drilling, there's
labor, there's chemicals, there's grease,

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:59.100
there's all sorts of other stuff that are
considered intangible.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:05.020
These expenses normally are 60 to 80 percent
of the total cost of drilling a well, and

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:09.020
they are 100 percent deductible in the year
incurred.

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:14.550
So if it costs three hundred thousand dollars
to drill a well and we say well of that, three

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:18.480
hundred thousand seventy five percent is intangible.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:24.410
The investor gets a deduction right away of
the full amount.

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:30.120
Even if you don't produce oil, even if you
don't strike oil, as long as you started to

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:36.290
operate that facility by the 31st of March
of the following year, you get to deduct all

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:39.060
this stuff regardless of what's actually happening.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:41.580
Most businesses don't get to do that.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:49.360
Oil and gas also get accelerated depreciation
of infrastructure and they also get an exemption

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:51.130
on alternative minimum tax.

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:52.790
And I know we're getting sort of like wonky.

00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:56.110
And here there's something called alternative
minimum tax.

00:05:56.110 --> 00:06:03.039
The point of alternative minimum tax is to
avoid people from being able to deduct all

00:06:03.039 --> 00:06:04.800
the way down to owing nothing.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:12.160
So at higher incomes, imagine you make five
million dollars you might be able to by combining

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:18.280
accelerated depreciation, intangible deductions,
all this different stuff, you might be able

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:20.130
to say, hey, you know what?

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:23.870
I have an adjusted income of only a million
dollars, even though there was a five million

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:27.620
dollars that came in, I've got all these different
deductions, I get down to a million dollars.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:33.200
The point of alternative minimum tax is to
say you can't deduct yourself below a certain

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.060
level of taxable income.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:44.169
The tax law specifically exempts those intangible
drilling costs from alternative minimum tax.

00:06:44.169 --> 00:06:52.150
So regardless of your amte, you can always
continue to deduct out those intangible drilling

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:53.150
costs.

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:55.650
That's a gift to oil and gas.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:07:02.560
So nice job by Katie Porter pointing out that
there are special exceptions made for oil

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:05.789
and gas that don't apply to other businesses.

00:07:05.789 --> 00:07:07.630
And I could go on with even more of these.

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:09.930
There are actually a ton of them.

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:15.570
And the whole point, the oil and gas industry
doesn't want you to know about this, because

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:17.300
if you did, you might be outraged, as you
should be

